<!--
    kuna issue testcase: the Listing walk's unmapped-CALL-target entry
    suppression (`option unmappedentry on|off`, default-ON).

    Issue:      kuna-analysis-unmappedentry - `kuna functions` on
                crackmes.one/66fd63059b533b4c22bd0b91 (32-bit ELF,
                "Overlapping / misaligned instructions") reported
                sub_fe6dca9f at 0xfe6dca9f, size 0, in a 25 KB image whose
                highest mapped byte is 0x7000.  The address is the target of
                a `call` encoded one byte early behind an always-taken
                `xor eax,eax; je +1`.
    Stage:      P1 code/data partition - the Listing tier's recursive-descent
                walk (`listing/walk.rs`), function-worklist admission.
    P0 assert:  `option unmappedentry on|off`.

    The Listing tier is built ONLY on the real-object bootstrap path, never on
    this XML binaryimage path (docs/spec/01-program-prep.md section 1.6), which is
    exactly why the parity oracle is structurally untouched by this change.  On
    this path the option is therefore INERT, and that is what this case pins -
    the same contract `kuna-listing-flag.xml` pins for `option listing`:

      * `option unmappedentry off` PARSES and round-trips (an unknown option
        would make the console error and the second decompile would not run),
      * flipping it changes NOTHING about the rendered C, in both directions.

    The end-to-end two-pass proof (option off = the phantom entry, default =
    no phantom) needs a real object and lives in the cargo gate
    `decompiler/crates/kuna-console/tests/verify_unmappedentry.rs`, over the
    `unmapped_call_x86_64` fixture: the same overlapping-call shape reduced to
    22 bytes of .text.

    Reuses the GH-558 bytes as a small, deterministic loadable function.

    Source:           int gh558(int x) { if (x LE 8) return 1; return 0; }
    Compiled (as):    cmp $8,%edi ; jg +6 ; mov $1,%eax ; ret ; xor %eax,%eax ; ret
-->
